      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coleman was originally established in the 1890s as a bustling trading post called Ego in the Choctaw Nation of Indian Territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coleman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially changed its name from Ego to Coleman in 1910 to honor a railroad official who surveyed the site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coleman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The local economy in Coleman centers on farming, producing livestock and renowned local sweet potatoes in fertile sandy and black soils.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coleman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "An annual Ole Settlers Reunion brings former residents back to Coleman to celebrate the town's heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coleman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Coleman Public Schools trace their roots back to a one-room schoolhouse in the original settlement of Ego.",
